=== factory eval
Run the fixtures matrix and write a scorecard

Usage:
  factory eval [flags]
  factory eval [command]

Available Commands:
  check       Lint the fixtures; exit 1 when one is malformed
  compare     Compare two sets of trajectory files offline
  diff        Compare two scorecards; exit 1 when the new one is worse
  replay      Print the spans a run recorded, gate spans with their triggered flag
  run         Run one split of the fixtures matrix and write a scorecard
  splits      Print pass rate per split, dev and holdout side by side
  stats       Summarise trajectory files offline

Flags:
      --agent string      agent preset name, never a model name (required)
      --arm strings       arms to run: preset, control (bare-bash baseline) (default [preset])
      --fixtures string   directory of fixtures (default "evals/fixtures")
      --format string     edit format axis, e.g. diff or whole
  -h, --help              help for eval
      --out string        directory the scorecard is written to (default "evals")
      --packs string      packs lock file the run used, e.g. factory.lock
      --redo              rerun tickets --run-dir already finished
      --replay            re-score the outputs stored in --run-dir without dispatching
      --run-dir string    keep results and trajectories here so a killed run resumes
      --split string      samples to run: dev (default) or holdout; holdout runs only when named (default "dev")
      --tries int         tries per ticket; the scorecard gets pass_rate_1..N (default 1)
      --workflow string   workflow name (required)

Use "factory eval [command] --help" for more information about a command.

=== factory eval check
Checks every fixture for its required file and keys, its seed, and a hidden directory per ticket.
--fix writes back only what the tree determines: the name and the seed key.

Usage:
  factory eval check [flags]

Flags:
      --fix               write back the data the tree determines
      --fixtures string   directory of fixtures (default "evals/fixtures")
  -h, --help              help for check

=== factory eval compare
Compare two sets of trajectory files offline

Usage:
  factory eval compare <old> <new> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for compare

=== factory eval diff
Prints pass rate, main green rate, tokens per merged PR and median wall time with deltas.
Exits 1 when the new scorecard is worse on pass rate or on tokens per merged PR (more than 10% up).

Usage:
  factory eval diff <old.json> <new.json> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for diff

=== factory eval replay
Print the spans a run recorded, gate spans with their triggered flag

Usage:
  factory eval replay <run.trace.jsonl> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for replay

=== factory eval run
Same as the bare eval command. --split dev (the default) runs the dev samples. Holdout samples run only with --split holdout.

Usage:
  factory eval run [flags]

Flags:
      --agent string      agent preset name, never a model name (required)
      --arm strings       arms to run: preset, control (bare-bash baseline) (default [preset])
      --fixtures string   directory of fixtures (default "evals/fixtures")
      --format string     edit format axis, e.g. diff or whole
  -h, --help              help for run
      --out string        directory the scorecard is written to (default "evals")
      --packs string      packs lock file the run used, e.g. factory.lock
      --redo              rerun tickets --run-dir already finished
      --replay            re-score the outputs stored in --run-dir without dispatching
      --run-dir string    keep results and trajectories here so a killed run resumes
      --split string      samples to run: dev (default) or holdout; holdout runs only when named (default "dev")
      --tries int         tries per ticket; the scorecard gets pass_rate_1..N (default 1)
      --workflow string   workflow name (required)

=== factory eval splits
Reads one or more scorecards, puts each ticket in dev or holdout by the hash of its sample id, and prints both pass rates in one table.

Usage:
  factory eval splits <scorecard.json>... [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for splits

=== factory eval stats
Summarise trajectory files offline

Usage:
  factory eval stats <trajectories> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for stats

=== factory first-run
Audit this repo and show what the factory would fix, at no cost

Usage:
  factory first-run [flags]

Flags:
      --annotations string   reason-tagged suppressions file; the default is optional (default ".factory/annotations.yml")
      --dry-run              stop after the report; say what would be dispatched
      --fix                  dispatch one build for the first covered finding and open a draft pull request
      --gh string            also audit this GitHub repo (owner/repo)
  -h, --help                 help for first-run
      --sarif string         also write the findings to this file as SARIF 2.1.0

=== factory init
Write .factory/factory.yaml from a tier preset

Usage:
  factory init [flags]

Flags:
      --accessible          plain numbered prompts instead of the full-screen form
      --dir string          repo root to write into (default ".")
      --dispatcher string   dispatcher: local, claude-cloud, fire; default from the tier
      --force               overwrite an existing .factory/factory.yaml
  -h, --help                help for init
      --repo string         owner/name; default from the origin remote
      --tier string         tier preset: solo, startup, scaleup, enterprise, regulated, oss
      --tracker string      tracker: github, linear, jira
      --yes                 run without prompts; needs --tier and --tracker

=== factory logs
Print what a ticket's session is doing

Usage:
  factory logs <ticket> [flags]

Flags:
  -f, --follow              keep polling until the session ends
  -h, --help                help for logs
      --interval duration   poll interval for --follow (default 5s)
      --json                print one JSON event per line

=== factory mcp
Serve the factory to Claude Code over stdio, or over HTTP with --http (MCP)

Usage:
  factory mcp [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for mcp
      --http string   serve streamable HTTP at /mcp on this address (default host 127.0.0.1); the bearer token is read from FACTORY_MCP_TOKEN
      --read-only     serve only the read tools; write tools are refused

=== factory packs
Inspect the packs pinned in factory.lock

Usage:
  factory packs [command]

Available Commands:
  install     Install a pack from a signed catalogue
  list        Print each lock entry
  match       List the skill packs whose paths or keywords match
  migrate     Run a migration pack pinned in factory.lock over the repository
  run         Run a command and retry it while the manifest's success checks fail
  upgrade     Bump pinned packs to their newest upstream when the eval says it is not worse
  verify      Re-hash the cache against factory.lock

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for packs

Use "factory packs [command] --help" for more information about a command.

=== factory packs install
Install a pack from a signed catalogue

Usage:
  factory packs install <name>[@version] [flags]

Flags:
      --catalogue string   catalogue file or URL; its signature is read from <catalogue>.sig
  -h, --help               help for install
      --pubkey string      base64 ed25519 public key (default: $FACTORY_PACKS_PUBKEY)
      --root string        repository root holding factory.lock and .factory/cache (default ".")

=== factory packs list
Print each lock entry

Usage:
  factory packs list [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for list
      --json          print JSON
      --root string   repository root holding factory.lock and .factory/cache (default ".")

=== factory packs match
List the skill packs whose paths or keywords match

Usage:
  factory packs match <dir> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help           help for match
      --path strings   a file the task touches (repeatable)
      --text string    the task text

=== factory packs migrate
Run a migration pack pinned in factory.lock over the repository

Usage:
  factory packs migrate <owner/repo#path> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for migrate
      --root string   repository root holding factory.lock and .factory/cache (default ".")

=== factory packs run
Run a command and retry it while the manifest's success checks fail

Usage:
  factory packs run <pack.yaml> -- <command> [args] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for run
      --root string   repository root holding factory.lock and .factory/cache (default ".")
      --unlocked      run a pack that is not pinned in factory.lock (local authoring only)

=== factory packs upgrade
Bump pinned packs to their newest upstream when the eval says it is not worse

Usage:
  factory packs upgrade [flags]

Flags:
      --baseline string    scorecard JSON of the pinned version
      --cache string       directory for cached bare repositories (default: factory-packs under the system temp dir)
      --candidate string   scorecard JSON of the new version
      --dir string         directory holding factory.lock (default ".")
      --dry-run            report what would change and write nothing
  -h, --help               help for upgrade
      --remote string      base URL packs are cloned from (default "https://github.com")
      --tolerance float    largest allowed rise in tokens per merged PR, as a fraction (default 0.05)

=== factory packs verify
Re-hash the cache against factory.lock

Usage:
  factory packs verify [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for verify
      --root string   repository root holding factory.lock and .factory/cache (default ".")

=== factory plan
Turn a goal, epic or spec into a ticket DAG; --approve files it

Usage:
  factory plan [goal] [flags]

Flags:
      --approve           file the tickets; without it nothing is written
      --epic n            read the spec and tickets from epic issue n
  -h, --help              help for plan
      --json              print the DAG as JSON
      --repo-dir string   the repository the agent reads when drafting from a goal (default: here)
      --spec file         read the spec and tickets from file

